  "courseName": "Introductory Molecular Biology (200-level Undergraduate Biology/Biochemistry)",
  "estimatedLength": "35 minutes",
  "objectives": [
    "Identify the structural components of DNA, including the sugar-phosphate backbone, nitrogenous bases, and hydrogen bonds, and explain how they form the double helix.",
    "Apply complementary base-pairing rules (A-T and G-C) to infer strand sequence and relate pair specificity to hydrogen-bonding patterns.",
    "Describe the antiparallel orientation of DNA strands and connect 5'-to-3' backbone directionality to helix structure.",
    "Trace the functions of helicase, single-strand binding proteins, DNA polymerase, and DNA ligase at the replication fork.",
    "Explain why leading and lagging strands are synthesized differently and how antiparallel templates create Okazaki fragments.",
    "Predict the semiconservative outcome of DNA replication after one and two rounds of replication."
  ],
